body:
using [[Structure]]s is a better aproach for sorting and swaping

Parallel arrays are two or more [[Array]]s where position *i* in each one describes the same
thing:

```c++
char  names[SIZE][NAME_LEN];
int   ids[SIZE];
double gpas[SIZE];
// names[3], ids[3], gpas[3] are all student 3
```

Nothing in the code says they are related. The relationship lives only in the [[Index]] and in
your head, which is why the sorting case is the argument against them: swapping two students
means three separate swaps, and forgetting one silently scrambles the data. That is a
[[Logic Errors|logic error]] with no symptom until much later.

The [[Structure]] version keeps the fields together, so one assignment moves the whole record:

```c++
struct Student {
	char name[NAME_LEN];
	int id;
	double gpa;
};
Student roster[SIZE];

Student tmp = roster[i];    // one swap, not three
roster[i] = roster[j];
roster[j] = tmp;
```

Same data, one invariant the compiler enforces instead of one you have to remember. The general
version of the argument is cohesion in [[Coupling and Cohesion]]: things that change together
belong together.

Parallel arrays are still worth recognising, because they are what a lot of older C code and
plenty of file formats actually look like.

